@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changes
+- `evlog/cli.py`: added `evlog report --since DATE`. It accepts any ISO date or datetime and counts only events at or after it (inclusive). An invalid value gives a normal argparse error (exit code 2, no traceback).
+- Timestamps in the data mix offset-aware and naive values. Naive ones, and a `--since` without an offset, are treated as UTC. Comparing naive with aware datetimes would otherwise raise `TypeError`. `--since 2024-03-10` therefore means 2024-03-10T00:00Z.
+- `tests/test_report.py`: added tests for a datetime with an offset, the inclusive boundary with naive-as-UTC, no matches (prints `total 0`), and an invalid date.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest`: 6 tests, all OK. This includes the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`.
+- `python -m evlog report /dev/null --since foo` printed a one-line usage error. `--since 2024-03-10` on an empty file printed `total 0`.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+- Treating naive timestamps as UTC is my assumption. If ops logs are in local time, results near day boundaries will differ.
+- Only the `--since` value gets friendly error handling. A missing file or malformed JSON lines still raise tracebacks, as before.
+- Tested on Python 3.14 only. `datetime.fromisoformat` accepts fewer formats on Python versions before 3.11.
